<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas Arvis [Laws of the Evening Prayer]<br>Halachah 4, Part 4 (§§ 29–39)<br>Based on <span class="lr">LM I:65</span> (<i>"Vayomer Boaz el Rus"</i>)</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 29                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 29</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one studies <span class="h">Sefer Mishneh Torah</span> [Deuteronomy] on the night of <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>. For <span class="h">Mishneh Torah</span> is the aspect of binding the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> well, in the aspect of a seal within a seal, so that we never forget Him. For the Torah is the aspect of a seal — for through it one binds the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> in our hearts well. For the essential knowledge of Hashem is through the Torah, which is drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, which enters and is bound in the heart through the holy Torah, as mentioned above. But even the Torah itself one must review well, so as not to forget it, so that we do not forget Him, G-d forbid.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the great warning that Moshe Rabbainu, peace upon him, warned us: <span class="v">"only guard yourself and guard your soul exceedingly, lest you forget the things... and lest they depart from your heart all the days of your life, and you shall make them known to your children and your children's children."</span> And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Mishneh Torah</span> — the aspect of a seal within a seal. For the Torah is the aspect of a seal, being called the Torah of Truth, which is the aspect of a seal — for <span class="mx">"the seal of the Holy One blessed be He is truth."</span> Therefore at the beginning of the Torah, <span class="h">Emes</span> [truth] is imprinted, as it is written: <span class="v">"<b>B</b>ereishis bara <b>E</b>lokim"</span> — the final letters spell <span class="h">Emes</span> — which is the aspect of a seal.</div>

<div class="p">And <span class="h">Mishneh Torah</span> is the aspect of a seal upon a seal. For Moshe Rabbainu, peace upon him, repeated and reviewed the Torah before Israel in order to bind the seal of Torah in the heart of Israel well, in the aspect of a seal upon a seal. Therefore most of the words of <span class="h">Mishneh Torah</span> speak of this matter: Moshe warning Israel with many warnings upon warnings, that they should remember well all that Hashem did with them — all the great and awesome things that their eyes saw. As is clear throughout the entire <span class="h">Sefer Mishneh Torah</span>, and especially at its end, which is <span class="h">Shiras Ha'azinu</span>. For all the words of this song are so that the Torah will not be forgotten from the mouths of our seed, as it is written: <span class="v">"this song shall testify before him as a witness, for it shall not be forgotten from the mouth of his seed."</span> Therefore one recites <span class="h">Mishneh Torah</span> on the night of <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, for the entire essence of <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span> is to bind the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> well in our hearts, in the aspect of a seal within a seal, so that we never forget Him — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Mishneh Torah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 30                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 30</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Sefer Tehilim</span> that one recites then. This is the aspect of the tikun of prayer that one merits at the completion of the tikun on <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, as mentioned above — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Tehilim</span>, which encompasses all prayers, songs and praises. For we now merit to say many songs and praises, all of which are encompassed in <span class="h">Sefer Tehilim</span>. For we merit through the tikunim mentioned above to make one unity out of the entire prayer — through which one can speak many prayers, which are the entire <span class="h">Sefer Tehilim</span> composed by Dovid HaMelech, peace upon him, who saturated the Holy One blessed be He with many songs and praises, all encompassed in <span class="h">Sefer Tehilim</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the ten <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span>s with which <span class="h">Sefer Tehilim</span> concludes — <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span> at the beginning and <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span> at the end. This is the aspect of the tikun of prayer: when standing at the last word of prayer, one is still standing at the first word, as mentioned above. This is the aspect of <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span> at the beginning, <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span> at the end — for it is all one, as mentioned above. Therefore one says these ten <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span>s every day in <span class="h">Pesukei d'Zimrah</span> before prayer, for the essential tikun of prayer is through making one unity out of the entire prayer, that when standing at the end of the word etc. — which is the aspect of the ten <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span>s: <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span> at the beginning, <span class="v">"Halleluyah"</span> at the end, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 31                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 31</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Simchas Torah</span>. For the essential drawing of Torah from the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above, is through joy — which is the vessel for receiving Torah, as mentioned above. Therefore, after all the tikunim mentioned above are completed at the end of the days of <span class="h">Sukkos</span>, on <span class="h">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, we then celebrate <span class="h">Simchas Torah</span>. For this is the essential completion of the tikun: we merit to draw the aspect of receiving the Torah in perfection, and the essential receiving is through joy, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore we then complete the Torah and immediately begin [again], binding and joining its end with its beginning. This is the aspect mentioned above of the tikun of prayer: when standing at the end, at the last word, one is still standing at the beginning, at the first word. For it is entirely one, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">hakafos</span> [circuits] that we make with the <span class="h">Sefer Torah</span>. This is the aspect of encirclings and circles — the aspect of the <span class="h">machol</span> [dance/circle] that the Holy One blessed be He will make for the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in the future. For the <span class="h">machol</span> is dancing in a circle. And this is the aspect of "entirely one" mentioned above: the end joins with the beginning. This is the aspect of a circle, for in a circle no beginning or end is discernible. And this is the essential joy: the light of oneness is revealed — through which the end is included and joined with the beginning, and this is the aspect of a circle, the aspect of the <span class="h">machol</span> mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 32                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 32</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the greatness of traveling to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. And this is the aspect of what we find: many <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and upright people travel to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> and return, and sometimes travel and return several times, as we have seen in our generations from several <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and upright people. And so we find with our first father Avraham, peace upon him, who was the first to reveal the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. Hashem said to him: <span class="v">"go for yourself, from your land... to the land"</span> etc. — and this was one of his trials. And immediately upon coming to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, Hashem caused him to leave, as it is written: <span class="v">"there was a famine in the land, and Avram went down to Egypt"</span> etc. And even the departure from <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> to outside the Land was one of his trials. As Rashi explains there: to test whether he would question the words of the Holy One blessed be He, Who had told him to go to the land of Canaan, and now causes him to leave.</div>

<div class="p">And in truth it is a great trial. For it is wondrous, at first glance, that since <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> is so holy and awesome, and Hashem Himself commanded Avraham to uproot himself from his land and birthplace and father's house to go to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> — and now, when he arrives, He causes events that force him to leave such a holy and awesome place. But in truth, according to the above, the matter is well understood. For in truth the essential tikun of all the worlds and all the souls is in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> specifically, for there is the essential revelation of His G-dliness — which is the essential tikun and <span class="h">tachlis</span>: to recognize Him in truth. For the essential gazing upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> mentioned above — is in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> specifically, where the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> and the <span class="h">Kodesh Hakodashim</span> are, where the point of the <span class="h">Even HaSh'siyah</span> is, where the <span class="h">Kohen Gadol</span> enters the innermost sanctum on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — where the essential <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> resides, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">But it is impossible to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> and merit there the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, except by first repairing vessels to receive there the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> — from where the essential attainment of <span class="h">da'as</span> and Torah derives, as mentioned above. And it is impossible to make and repair the vessels except through the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above. And on account of this, it is very difficult to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, for this is the aspect of the <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> work mentioned above — the essential toil being that this depends on that, and where does one begin? — as explained above at length. For the tikun of the vessels depends on the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, and it is impossible to come to the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> except through vessels, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And from this derive all the great and formidable obstacles that confront every person before coming to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. And whoever is greater, his obstacles are greater still. Until the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> have immeasurably intense obstacles that are difficult to break. For the essential gathering of all the dispersed — which are the aspect of all the sparks that fell and were scattered among the <span class="h">klipos</span> — their essential tikun and gathering is to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. As it is written: <span class="v">"if your dispersed are at the end of the heavens, from there Hashem your G-d will gather you and from there He will take you, and Hashem your G-d will bring you to the Land"</span> etc. And it is written: <span class="v">"and it shall be on that day, a great shofar will be blown, and the lost in the land of Ashur and the dispersed in the land of Egypt shall come, and they shall bow to Hashem on the holy mountain in Yerushalayim."</span></div>

<div class="p">And it is impossible for the <span class="h">tzadik</span> to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> except through the gathering of the dispersed. But these dispersed sparks and souls are impossible to gather except through the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. For the dispersed sparks and souls are drawn from the Breaking of Vessels — when holy sparks fell into the depths of the <span class="h">klipos</span>. And Adam HaRishon, had he not sinned by eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil and had he prayed his prayer, would have repaired them. And through his sin — and especially because he then also flawed the holy <span class="h">Bris</span>, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="mx">"Adam HaRishon was drawn in his foreskin"</span> — as brought in the Writings of the Ari"zal, see there — through this, not only did he not elevate and repair the fallen sparks from the Breaking of Vessels, but he caused them to fall even further. And so each person, through his sins, scatters and causes the sparks to fall further, may the Merciful One save us.</div>

<div class="p">And the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of the highest level are engaged in every generation in repairing and elevating these sparks and dispersed souls — which is the essential toil and immense labor of the <span class="h">Ba'al HaSadeh</span> mentioned above: to repair the dispersed souls, to bring them into the holy Field. But their essential tikun is through the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, where the essential gazing upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> resides, as mentioned above — which is the essential tikun that the <span class="h">Ba'al HaSadeh</span> draws, as mentioned above. But it is impossible to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> until one elevates these dispersed ones and gathers them to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, as mentioned above. For in truth the essential tikun of the vessels, in order to receive the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span> and to attain His G-dliness, is through the elevation of the dispersed sparks mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">As is understood from the Writings of the Ari"zal: the tikun of the vessels and worlds anew is through elevating the sparks that fell from the depths of the <span class="h">klipos</span>, and drawing abundant light from above — the aspect of the new light of <span class="h">Mah</span>. And through this one causes a supernal unification through the aspect of the elevation of <span class="h">Mayin Nukvin</span>. And through this the worlds and vessels are repaired etc. And the essential tikun is in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, as mentioned above. But how does one merit to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>? For this depends on that: it is impossible to elevate these sparks except by drawing abundant light from above — which is drawn from the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> and the gazing toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, as mentioned above — which one merits in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> specifically. And to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> it is impossible to come except through the tikun of the vessels made through elevating these sparks, as mentioned above. And if so, where is the beginning?</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the obstacles intensify greatly before coming to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. For the <span class="h">klipos</span> and <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> that grip these fallen sparks — the dispersed souls — intensify with formidable obstacles against one who wishes to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> to extract the souls and sparks from them. Therefore one must strengthen oneself exceedingly to break the formidable obstacles with self-sacrifice. And then, through the self-sacrifice of giving up one's life to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, some illumination from the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> is drawn upon him even outside the Land — so that he has the power to break the <span class="h">klipos</span> from which the obstacles derive, and to elevate holy sparks from there. And through this he has the power to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, to be nullified there in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>, to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, and to receive from there wondrous and awesome illuminations and attainments of Torah — through the vessels he merited to repair by elevating the sparks, through breaking the obstacles, through the greatness of his self-sacrifice to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one generally needs to return from <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. For since the entire time of exile — as long as Moshiach Tzidkainu has not yet come — it is still impossible to clarify all the sparks in perfection, as is known from the Writings. And the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, even though he clarified and repaired and elevated many sparks through his journey to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> through his immense self-sacrifice, nevertheless many still remain outside the Land that must be elevated and clarified. And it is impossible to clarify them except by first being in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore he must return from <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> to outside the Land — in order to go back and clarify and elevate and repair all the sparks and dispersed, fallen souls. For now specifically he can repair and elevate them, through the light of the holiness of the <span class="h">bitul</span> — which is the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> — that he merited to receive there. Through this is their essential tikun and elevation, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this itself is the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>. The <span class="h">tzadik</span> runs with self-sacrifice to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, and afterward — <span class="h">v'shov</span>: he returns from there. And all this is for the tikun of the worlds, for the elevation of the sparks and souls that fell through the aspect of the Breaking of Vessels caused by their sins, may the Merciful One save us. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"the <i>Chayos</i> ran and returned, like the appearance of a flash."</span> <span class="v">"Like the appearance of a flash"</span> specifically — which is the aspect of clay vessels that were broken. Meaning: corresponding to the aspect of the Breaking of Vessels — which is the aspect of the appearance of a flash — so too one must return from the <span class="h">bitul</span> many times in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>. This is the aspect of returning from <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, where the essential <span class="h">bitul</span> resides. And this is: <span class="v">"the <i>Chayos</i> ran and returned, like the appearance of a flash"</span> — one must receive the vitality from the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span> in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> many times, <span class="v">"like the appearance of a flash."</span> And Rashi explains: <span class="mx">"like light that emerges from between clay shards"</span> — which is the light of the sparkling of the sparks that fell in the aspect of the Breaking of Vessels, as mentioned above. Meaning: corresponding to the appearance of the flash — corresponding to the sparks that fell in the Breaking of Vessels. For it is impossible to elevate the sparks all at once. Therefore one must go back and ascend many times in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, in order to repair vessels each time to elevate them gradually and in measure, in order. For the more sparks one elevates, the more vessels are repaired. And the more vessels are repaired, the more sparks one can elevate. For this depends on that, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 33                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 33</div>

<div class="p">And on account of this, Moshe Rabbainu consented to the mission of the spies. And this was the mistake of Israel, who wanted to send spies to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. For they saw that it is impossible to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> except through the gathering of the dispersed — the fallen sparks — and it is impossible to elevate them except through the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. And if so, how is it possible to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>? Therefore they devised a plan to send spies — who were great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — so that they, in their holiness, would break the obstacles and come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> and receive there the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, and afterward return from there immediately. And with this power, Israel would be able to repair vessels to elevate the remaining sparks — since they would already have the power to draw upon themselves the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> through the spies who had already been there and returned. And through this, all of Israel could come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> easily.</div>

<div class="p">And in truth, it is seemingly a wondrous plan. But then the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> prevailed with an exceedingly great intensification — since it saw that all of Israel wished to enter and conquer <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, which would have elevated all the sparks and subdued the <span class="h">klipos</span> and <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> completely. Therefore it prevailed greatly upon the spies, until they were drawn after it to bring an evil report about the holy Land. And through this they caused what they caused: weeping for generations, the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, and all the exiles.</div>

<div class="p">For in truth, Israel erred in this. For if they themselves — six hundred thousand holy souls — did not have the power to break the obstacles to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, how would twelve spies have the power to break the intensity of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>? On the contrary: they should have given up their lives, all of them, to go to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> without any spies, only with actual self-sacrifice. And then, through their self-sacrifice, they would certainly have broken all the obstacles, as mentioned above, and would have come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> in peace.</div>

<div class="p">And on account of their flaw, it was caused that even Moshe Rabbainu was unable to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. For Moshe Rabbainu, according to the essence of his greatness and awesome level — had he come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> he would have elevated all the sparks and all the souls and repaired all the worlds, and without this he could not enter. But on account of the sin of the spies mentioned above — through which it was decreed upon the entire generation not to enter <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> — through this, Moshe did not have the power to elevate all the sparks and souls. Therefore it was caused that he flawed at <span class="h">Mai Merivah</span> [the Waters of Strife] — which is the aspect of the flaw in the drawing of the Torah drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>: he did not draw the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span> and Torah from there as was fitting for his immense level, in a manner that would enable us to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. And through this he was unable to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For Moshe could not come with the intention of returning from there, due to the essence of his immense holiness. For had he come there to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> and been included and nullified there in the place where he would have been included and nullified, and had he repaired what he would have repaired — he would not have been able to return from there to outside the Land. And had Moshe Rabbainu remained there, it would have been necessary to elevate there all the fallen sparks in the world — from which the vessels to receive the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span> in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> are fashioned — and the time for this had not yet come. Therefore Hashem decreed upon him not to enter <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. He would only see it from afar. And through the seeing itself he would draw the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> to outside the Land, so that Israel in every generation would have the power to clarify sparks, gather the dispersed, and come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Hashem causes it that in every generation, children of Israel go to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> and return. For now, before the coming of Moshiach, it is impossible to clarify the sparks except through this aspect: going to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> and returning — in order to clarify the remaining sparks, which cannot be clarified except by first going to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> and returning from there, as mentioned above. And this is done in every generation through the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and their people, until Moshiach Tzidkainu comes and completes the clarification in perfection. And then we will all return to our land and dwell there forever. For then the clarification of all the sparks will truly be completed. And the verse will be fulfilled in perfection: <span class="v">"if your dispersed are... Hashem your G-d will bring you to the Land"</span> etc. — speedily in our days, amain.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 34                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 34</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of all the exiles of the entirety of Israel. For we have already been in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> several times and returned from there during the exiles. And all of this is the aspect mentioned above. For because the sparks fell into the depths of the <span class="h">klipos</span>, they must be clarified through many strategies. And the essential thing is the beginning, for all beginnings are difficult. And this is on account of the above: because this depends on that, and one does not know where the beginning lies. For it is impossible to clarify them except through <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, and it is impossible to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> except through clarifying them, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And so it is with all the beginnings that one must make to return to Hashem. For it is impossible to arouse oneself in <span class="h">teshuvah</span> except by having some light of Hashem sparkle upon him — to arouse him to return. And it is impossible for His light to shine upon him except when he returns in <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — for <span class="mx">"no stranger shall eat of the holy."</span> And if so, where is the beginning? And sometimes, when Hashem has mercy on him and shines His light into the depths of his darkness and his great fall in order to arouse him to <span class="h">teshuvah</span> — he is unable to receive this light. For according to the extent of his shattering into fragments upon fragments through his evil deeds, everything is in the aspect of excessive light for him.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore in truth, it generally happens that a person is aroused to <span class="h">teshuvah</span> for some time, and afterward falls from it. And this may happen many times. And this is the aspect of all the exiles collectively for Israel: when they came to <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span> they fell even more, because they did not guard themselves to sanctify themselves sufficiently, as befits the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>. For in the king's palace one certainly must be careful to be in ever-greater cleanliness and purity. And because they were not careful in this, everything was in the aspect of excessive light for them — through which the essential Breaking of Vessels occurs, from which the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> intensifies. Therefore they sinned there even more, until on account of our sins we were exiled from our land — this several times.</div>

<div class="p">But nevertheless, <span class="v">"the right hand of Hashem is exalted."</span> And <span class="v">"You, Hashem, are on high forever"</span> — and Rashi explains: <span class="v">"forever Your hand is uppermost."</span> For despite everything, in all the earlier redemptions when Israel was in <span class="h">Eretz Yisra'el</span>, each time they repaired many tikunim and many <span class="h">reshimos</span> remained — in order that there should be power to repair vessels to receive the light of the attainment of His G-dliness. For even though afterward, when they sinned, all the tikunim they had previously repaired were damaged even further, nevertheless many wondrous <span class="h">reshimos</span> remained from them. And through this power we live in exile, and we have the power to receive some sparkling from the knowledge of His G-dliness and His holy faith.</div>

<div class="p">And so it is with every person at every time. Even though one begins innumerable times in the service of Hashem and falls each time — even if one's fall is very great, may the Merciful One save us, even one who falls, G-d forbid, into actual transgressions, may the Merciful One save us — nevertheless, everything that one began, every beginning each time, is never lost. For even though at the time of the fall everything is ruined, nevertheless a <span class="h">reshimah</span> always remains from every act of service and every beginning. And these <span class="h">reshimos</span> are wondrous tikunim, and they are needed and essential for the building of holiness that must be established.</div>

<div class="p">For we need now that Moshiach Tzidkainu should come — a complete redemption with no exile after it. And we need to establish a building of holiness, a complete building, in a wondrous manner that will never be destroyed. And we need to gather all the dispersed of Israel and collect them together in a second sanctification. And no spark or dispersed soul shall remain outside — for He in His mercy devises plans so that no one shall be banished from Him. Therefore this building requires innumerable vessels. And it requires immeasurably many tools of craftsmanship.</div>

<div class="p">As I heard once from the mouth of Rabbainu the holy one, of blessed memory, who said in these words: <span class="mx">"I can make wondrous vessels, but the difficulty is that I also need to make the tools of craftsmanship [<i>gitzayg</i>]."</span> Therefore now, in these generations of <span class="h">ikvesa d'meshicha</span> [the heels of Moshiach], when the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> has spread and intensified exceedingly in length and breadth — because it sees that very soon Moshiach will come and extract its plunder from its mouth — therefore whatever any member of Israel sometimes does, any act of holiness — whether Torah study, or prayer, or charity and acts of lovingkindness, or any other <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — everything is exceedingly, exceedingly precious in the eyes of Hashem. And it is exceedingly needed by the <span class="h">Ba'al HaSadeh</span> who is engaged in the tikun of the Field — the tikun of all the souls and all the worlds. For every act of holiness, great or small, is needed by him greatly for the building of holiness that he is engaged in building.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore even though this person who did the act of holiness, or already engaged somewhat in the service of Hashem for days or years, falls afterward — even if his fall is very great, may the Merciful One save us, and even if he was aroused again to the service of Hashem and fell again, and so it was innumerable times — even if it is however it is — nevertheless, know and believe with complete faith that no utterance and no small act of service is ever lost. And even a mere arousal and a good thought is never lost. As it says in the holy Zohar: <span class="mx">"no good will is ever lost"</span> etc. For immediately upon being aroused with any arousal at all — how much more so when performing any act of holiness — immediately the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> of every generation, who is engaged in the tikun of the Field, seizes it and places it where he places it, for the wondrous and awesome building he is engaged in building — to bring into it all the dispersed in the world, so that not one of them remains outside.</div>

<div class="p">As is understood from the words of Rabbainu of blessed memory in the Torah <span class="lr">"Tik'u" III (LM II:8)</span>, regarding the matter of the wondrous multitude of houses that are added without measure when one more participant is added to an act of holiness. See there, on the verse <span class="v">"let not the neighbor say: I am sick"</span> etc. It is understood and explained there: when there are, for example, twenty people engaged in prayer and one more joins them, then the houses of prayer multiply without measure. For when one letter is added to a combination of twenty letters, all the letters combine thousands upon thousands and myriads upon myriads of times — more than the mouth can speak and the heart can think etc. — see there. And Rabbainu of blessed memory was precise in calling the one who joins by the name <span class="v">"neighbor"</span> [<span class="h">shachein</span>] — as hinted in the verse <span class="v">"let not the <i>neighbor</i> say: I am sick."</span> <span class="v">"Neighbor"</span> specifically — like one who dwells as a neighbor near some householder, which is only temporary, and afterward in the course of time he leaves. So too, when a person is added to holiness, even though he is only in the aspect of a neighbor — not permanently established among them — nevertheless, through being a mere neighbor to an act of holiness, even temporarily, the houses of prayer multiply without measure or number etc. — as explained there. And immeasurable great delight is made above through him, even though afterward he distances himself, like the neighbor.</div>

<div class="p">And so we see tangibly: for example, when there are nine men and they need to say a <span class="h">davar shebikdushah</span> — <span class="h">Kaddish</span> or <span class="h">Kedushah</span> — even if they are all great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, they cannot say the <span class="h">Kedushah</span> with nine. And when one person joins them — even the most lowly of the lowly, from the marketplace, temporarily — and they count him for the <span class="h">minyan</span>, even though he immediately returns to the marketplace, nevertheless how much delight he caused to Hashem. For the entire <span class="h">Kedushah</span> was completed through him. And even if there was a <span class="h">minyan</span> without him, and even if there were many <span class="h">minyanim</span>, nevertheless whenever one more soul of Israel is added, His holiness and honor increase more and more — for <span class="v">"in the multitude of people is the king's glory."</span> Therefore immediately when one more person joins them, the holiness increases more and more. On the contrary: the more people join in holiness, when one more is added, he creates wondrous tikunim and buildings — far, far more — thousands upon thousands and myriads upon myriads without measure. As well understood in the Torah <span class="lr">"Tik'u"</span> mentioned above regarding "two stones build two houses, three [stones build six houses]... five, one hundred and twenty... seven, seven hundred and twenty" etc. — more than the mouth can speak and the heart can think. See there and understand well.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that whatever a person does — any act at all in His service — even though afterward one does what one does, G-d forbid — nevertheless this act is never lost and has already been joined to the wondrous building, and has caused a great tikun for the building of holiness, and thousands upon thousands and myriads of houses have been multiplied through it, as mentioned above. And if one strengthens oneself each time, all the days of one's life, to begin anew each time, regardless of what passes — then ultimately all these tikunim caused by every act of service and every beginning will be gathered, and all of them will gather to one's aid, to strengthen and fortify one to return to Hashem in truth. And <span class="v">"though your beginning was small, your end shall flourish greatly."</span> And then one will see what was accomplished through every act of holiness — that nothing is ever lost.</div>

<div class="p">And so it is with the entirety of Israel: all the tikunim accomplished through the earlier redemptions, even though they were later ruined through our sins — nevertheless wondrous <span class="h">reshimos</span> remained from them. And Moshiach Tzidkainu uses them for the final building he is engaged in building — all of which are essential to him. And when he completes his building and comes to redeem us, speedily in our days, then our eyes will see and our hearts will rejoice at the greatness of the tikunim accomplished through every redemption and every act of service, collectively and individually. As it is written: <span class="v">"I remember for you the lovingkindness of your youth, the love of your bridal days, your walking after Me in the wilderness"</span> etc. And it is written: <span class="v">"come and gaze from the peak of <i>Amanah</i>, from the peak of S'nir and Chermon, from the dens of lions, from the mountains of leopards."</span> The meaning is: in the end, Hashem will remember for us all the lovingkindnesses and merits we have from the very beginning — when we walked after Him in the wilderness, in an unsown land. And we passed through dens of lions and mountains of leopards etc.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that even though Israel afterward did what they did — until the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was destroyed through their sins and they were exiled from their land — and all these merits seemingly did not help, since they are so far distant in such exiles, in such troubles that have passed over Israel without measure, may the Merciful One save us. As it is written: <span class="v">"why do you say, Yaakov... 'my way is hidden from Hashem, and my judgment passes from my G-d'?"</span> And it is written: <span class="v">"You have indeed rejected and disgraced us"</span> etc. And it is written: <span class="v">"and Tziyon said: 'Hashem has forsaken me, my Lord has forgotten me.'"</span></div>

<div class="p">But in truth it is not so, as the verse concludes: <span class="v">"can a woman forget her suckling... even these may forget, but I will not forget you."</span> Meaning: ultimately we will return to Hashem in truth, and Hashem will forget all our evil deeds. But all the good deeds, every good motion, Hashem will not forget — in the aspect of <span class="v">"even these shall be forgotten, but I will not forget you,"</span> which our Sages of blessed memory expounded: the act of the Calf, when they said <span class="v">"these are your gods"</span> etc. — Hashem will forget. But the merit of receiving the Torah, when they accepted His G-dliness upon them, in the aspect of <span class="v">"I am Hashem your G-d"</span> — this Hashem will never forget.</div>

<div class="p">And so it is in particular with every person at every time: even though what passes over him passes — and it seems to him as if there is no benefit at all, G-d forbid, from what he occasionally began a little in the service of Hashem, since he sees how far he is — nevertheless, in the end he will see what he accomplished at the beginning, in the aspect of <span class="v">"come and gaze from the peak of <i>Amanah</i>,"</span> as mentioned above. Therefore one must always strengthen oneself all the days of one's life, to begin anew each time. For <span class="v">"the Lord will not cast off forever."</span> And <span class="mx">"no good will is ever lost."</span></div>

<div class="p">And as Rabbainu of blessed memory said on the verse <span class="v">"when its waves rise, You still them"</span> — that whenever a person lifts himself toward Hashem, even though he does not succeed, G-d forbid, in drawing close to Hashem, for he falls afterward, may the Merciful One save us — nevertheless, this very thing — that he strengthens himself and raises and lifts himself each time toward Hashem — this is his praise and his glory.</div>

<div class="p">May Hashem help us to always strengthen ourselves, until we merit to return to Him in truth, speedily, speedily. Amain, may it be His will.</div>

<div class="p" style="text-align:center;font-weight:600;margin-top:1.5rem;">And we return to the first matter — the matter of <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span>.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 35                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 35</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"he encountered the place and lodged there, for the sun had set"</span> etc. — and our Sages of blessed memory said: he established <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span>. And this is: <span class="v">"he took from the stones of the place and placed them around his head"</span> etc. For it is explained above that <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> is the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>: one nullifies oneself at night due to the intensity of the judgment — which is the aspect of the darkness of night, as mentioned above. And it is explained there that the essential tikun of prayer is through this aspect. For through this one makes one unity out of the entire prayer. For as long as one does not merit to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, the prayer is not in perfection — and every letter and utterance one speaks in prayer does not allow the person to speak further letters and utterances. For the utterance and the letter seize the person forcefully and embrace and clasp him and do not allow him to proceed further to speak more utterances etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is: <span class="v">"he took from the stones of the place and placed them around his head."</span> And our Sages of blessed memory expounded: <span class="mx">"the stones began quarreling with one another — this one saying: upon me let the <i>tzadik</i> rest his head"</span> etc. <span class="mx">"Immediately the Holy One blessed be He made them into one stone."</span> For the stones are letters, as is known — as stated in <span class="h">Sefer Yetzirah</span>: <span class="mx">"two stones build two houses, three stones"</span> etc. Meaning: before one merits the <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> that Yaakov Avinu established then — the letters and utterances do not allow the person to proceed from them to speak further, and each one says that the person should remain with them, as mentioned above. This is the aspect of the stones — which are the aspect of the letters — quarreling with one another: <span class="mx">"upon me let the <i>tzadik</i> rest his head."</span> Each letter desires that the <span class="h">tzadik</span> invest his entire head and thought into it alone, and remain there and not proceed to the other letters. And so with each letter.</div>

<div class="p">But through the merit and power of Yaakov Avinu — who merited then to be nullified toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> and to repair <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> — through this, Hashem performed a miracle and made from all the stones one stone. Meaning: from all the letters one letter was made. Meaning, as mentioned above: when one merits <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, one unity is made from all the utterances of prayer — even when standing at the last letter of prayer, one is still at the first letter, for it is entirely one. And this is the aspect of all the stones — which are the letters — being made into one stone, meaning one letter, for it is entirely one. This is the essential tikun of prayer that one merits through the <span class="h">bitul</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> that Yaakov Avinu established then, when he encountered that place, as mentioned above. And all this was at the place of the <span class="h">Even HaSh'siyah</span>, where the <span class="h">Kohen Gadol</span> enters the innermost sanctum on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, where the essential <span class="h">bitul</span> resides, as mentioned above. It emerges that <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> in the closing of the eyes etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 36                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 36</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the sleep of the night. For sleep is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>: one closes one's eyes and is included in the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span>, as is brought in the Torah <span class="lr">"Vay'hi Mikaitz — Zikaron" (LM I:54)</span> — see there. But one needs merit for this: that a person should merit during sleep to be nullified toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"who shall ascend the mountain of Hashem?"</span> etc. — which is said regarding the soul during sleep, as is stated in the holy Zohar. And this is: <span class="v">"who shall ascend the mountain of Hashem?"</span> specifically — meaning the aspect of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where the point of the <span class="h">Even HaSh'siyah</span> is, in the <span class="h">Kodesh Hakodashim</span>, where the essential <span class="h">bitul</span> resides, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And afterward, in the morning, is the awakening from sleep — this is the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>: the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span>. And then is the essential sweetening through the Torah received from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>. And this is the aspect of the light of the <span class="h">tefillin</span> that emerge in the morning — which are the aspect of the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above, the aspect of <span class="v">"set me as a seal upon your heart"</span> etc., as is known. For the essential receiving of Torah is by day, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="v">"on the day He commanded"</span> — <span class="mx">"this teaches that He spoke with him only by day."</span> For in the light of morning, this is the aspect of returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span> of the night, and then is the aspect of the drawing of Torah from the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above. And through this is the essential sweetening of judgments in perfection — as explained above: the essential sweetening is when returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span> of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, where the essential sweetening is in perfection.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential tikun of the order of prayer is at <span class="h">Shacharis</span>: then one multiplies praises and songs and arranges the entire order of prayer — <span class="h">Korbanos</span>, <span class="h">Pesukei d'Zimrah</span> etc. For the essential perfection of prayer is through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that one merits to receive at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas Shacharis</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 37                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 37</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span> that one must bless every day, which our Sages of blessed memory derived from the verse: <span class="v">"and now, Israel, <i>mah</i> [what] does Hashem your G-d ask of you, but to fear"</span> etc. — and our Sages of blessed memory expounded: <span class="mx">"do not read <i>mah</i> but <i>me'ah</i> [a hundred]."</span> For the one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span> — which are the entirety of prayer, the entirety of all songs and praises, all included ten by ten until reaching the aspect of one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span> — all this is drawn from the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, through which is the essential tikun of prayer, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of "do not read <span class="h">mah</span> but <span class="h">me'ah</span>": <span class="h">mah</span> is the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, from where one draws the light through the <span class="h">reshimah</span> — from the aspect of <span class="h">mah</span> to the aspect of <span class="h">me'ah</span>: the aspect of the one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span> whose essential tikun is through the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>. Through this one can multiply <span class="h">berachos</span> and thanksgivings and prayers and requests, all included in the aspect of one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span>, as mentioned above. And this is: <span class="v">"<i>mah</i> does Hashem your G-d ask of you, but to fear"</span> etc. For the essential <span class="h">bitul</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">mah</span>, through which one merits afterward to draw from <span class="h">mah</span> to <span class="h">me'ah</span> — all this one merits only through <span class="h">yirah</span> [fear], which is the aspect of judgment. For due to the fear of judgment, one nullifies oneself completely toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, until one merits afterward to draw the aspect of receiving Torah from the <span class="h">reshimah</span>. This is the aspect of making from <span class="h">mah</span> — <span class="h">me'ah</span>, as mentioned above (and as explained regarding this in the Torah <span class="lr">"Anochi," LM I:4</span>).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Yitzchak specifically found the hundred <span class="h">she'arim</span> [measures/gates]. As it is written: <span class="v">"Yitzchak sowed... in that year a hundredfold, and Hashem blessed him."</span> And it is stated in the <span class="h">seforim</span> that the hundred <span class="h">she'arim</span> are the aspect of the one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span>. And Yitzchak specifically merited to find and attain them. This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"<i>mah</i> does Hashem your G-d ask of you, but to fear."</span> For specifically through <span class="h">yirah</span> — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Pachad Yitzchak</span> [the Dread of Yitzchak] — through this specifically one merits the aspect of <span class="h">mah</span>. Meaning: "do not read <span class="h">mah</span> but <span class="h">me'ah</span>" — the aspect of one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span>, which are the aspect of one hundred <span class="h">she'arim</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 38                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 38</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the one hundred sounds of the <span class="h">shofar</span> that are blown on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. For the essential tikun of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above is on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, as mentioned above. And then one merits to draw from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> through the <span class="h">shofar</span> — from <span class="h">mah</span> to <span class="h">me'ah</span> — the aspect of one hundred sounds, which are the aspect of one hundred <span class="h">she'arim</span>, the aspect of one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span>. For because the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span> is exceedingly exalted and lofty, as mentioned above, therefore when returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span> and drawing the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above, one must bind it finely and well to the heart through many, many vessels — which are the aspect of the multitude of sounds of the <span class="h">shofar</span>, from which the aspect of one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span> are drawn, which are the aspect of one hundred sounds — so that this great light should not, G-d forbid, pass from our hearts, due to the greatness of its light, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what our Sages of blessed memory said: one should review one's chapter one hundred and one times, for the sake of memory. For the more one reviews one's chapter, each time the Torah becomes more included in prayer. For when one studies the chapter the first time, one does not yet understand it well and it has not entered the heart well. And when one studies and reviews it a second time, one understands it more and perceives within it what was not understood at first — which is the aspect of the attainment of the hidden, which is the aspect of prayer. (And as explained in the Torah <span class="lr">"Chosam b'soch Chosam," LM I:22</span>.) And so each time. It emerges that when one reviews one's chapter one hundred and one times, the Torah — which is the chapter one studies — receives the light of the perfection of prayer, which is the aspect of one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span>, from which the essential memory derives — through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> being drawn from <span class="h">mah</span> to <span class="h">me'ah</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore one will certainly remember the Torah well.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore in Torah study one needs specifically one hundred and one times, as our Sages of blessed memory said: <span class="mx">"one who reviews his chapter one hundred times is not comparable to one who reviews it one hundred and one times."</span> For the first time one studies the chapter counts only as Torah study. And from the second time onward, the more one studies, the study increasingly counts as prayer — for each time one perceives and attains the hidden aspect more, which is the aspect of turning prayer into Torah, and introducing the aspect of prayer — the hidden — into the Torah. Therefore each time the Torah increasingly becomes included and counted as prayer. And so one must review up to one hundred times — besides the first study — and then the perfection of the light of prayer encompassed in one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span> enters completely into the Torah study. And then one will certainly remember it well. For the essential memory is drawn through the aspect of the one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span>, which are the perfection of prayer — through making from <span class="h">mah</span> — <span class="h">me'ah</span>, whose essential perfection is through the aspect of the memory that one draws, as mentioned above. This is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, which is called <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span>, when one blows the one hundred sounds — which are the aspect of the one hundred <span class="h">berachos</span>, as explained above at length — see there.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 39                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 39</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis Reshus</span> [the Evening Prayer is optional]. For <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> is in the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, which is above Torah. For the essential receiving of Torah is from the aspect of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that illuminates when returning from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore <span class="h">Tefilas Arvis</span> is <span class="h">reshus</span>. For there, obligation does not apply — for it is in the aspect of above Torah, which is the aspect of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, as explained above.</div>

