<div class="ssn">§§ 29–31: Hoshana Rabbah Night (Mishneh Torah, Tehilim) → Simchas Torah</div>

<div class="p">Mishneh Torah on Hoshana Rabbah night = seal within a seal. Torah = seal (Emes = God's seal; Bereishis bara Elokim = final letters spell Emes). Mishneh Torah = Moshe reviewing Torah to bind the seal of Torah in Israel's hearts = seal upon seal. Most of Mishneh Torah consists of warnings to remember. Shiras Ha'azinu = "this song shall testify... it shall not be forgotten." Sefer Tehilim on Hoshana Rabbah = tikun of prayer = many songs. Ten Halleluyahs ending Tehilim = Halleluyah at beginning and end = the unity of prayer (last word still at first). Simchas Torah: joy = vessel for Torah. Completing and immediately beginning the Torah = end joins beginning = the circle. Hakafos = circles = machol (dance in a circle) = no beginning or end discernible = "entirely one."</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 32–34: Eretz Yisra'el, the Exiles, and "Nothing Is Ever Lost"</div>

<div class="p">Eretz Yisra'el = the place of ultimate bitul (Bais HaMikdash, Even HaSh'siyah, Kodesh Hakodashim). But arriving there requires vessels, and vessels require the holiness of Eretz Yisra'el itself — the same paradox as Rosh HaShanah. This generates immense obstacles. One breaks them through mesirus nefesh, which draws the holiness of Eretz Yisra'el even outside the Land. Tzadikim must generally return — before Moshiach, not all sparks can be clarified in one trip. Going and returning = ratzo v'shov on the level of Eretz Yisra'el itself. "The Chayos ran and returned like the appearance of a flash" = light from between broken shards = sparks from the Breaking of Vessels.</div>

<div class="p">The spies' sin: Israel's plan seemed clever (send twelve tzadikim to absorb the holiness, then return), but the Sitra Achra overwhelmed them. Israel should have gone with mesirus nefesh, without spies. Their sin caused Moshe's inability to enter — he could not have returned once there, and the time for the complete tikun had not arrived. He could only see from afar, drawing Eretz Yisra'el's holiness to Chutz LaAretz for all future generations.</div>

<div class="p">The great teaching of §34: NOTHING IS EVER LOST. Every beginning, every arousal, every small act of holiness — even from one who falls again and again — is immediately seized by the true tzadik and incorporated into the wondrous building. Rabbainu's teaching from LM II:8 ("Tik'u"): when one person joins twenty in prayer, the combinations multiply beyond all measure. Even a "neighbor" (temporary participant) creates immeasurable delight above. Even one who joins a minyan temporarily completes the Kedushah. "No good will is ever lost" (Zohar). "When its waves rise, You still them" = every lifting toward Hashem is itself one's praise and glory. "Though your beginning was small, your end shall flourish greatly." Rabbainu's holy words: "I can make wondrous vessels, but the difficulty is that I also need to make the tools of craftsmanship." All previous redemptions and exiles left reshimos; Moshiach uses them all. "Even these shall be forgotten, but I will not forget you" = the Calf will be forgotten, but "Anochi" will never be forgotten.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 35–36: Return to Arvis — Yaakov's Stones; Night and Morning</div>

<div class="p">"He took from the stones of the place" — the stones quarreled: "upon me let the tzadik rest his head" = before bitul, each letter/utterance seizes the person and won't let go. God made them one stone = one letter = the unity of prayer through bitul. This was at the Even HaSh'siyah. Sleep at night = bitul (closing eyes, included in the tachlis = Olam HaBa). "Who shall ascend the mountain of Hashem?" = the soul during sleep ascending to the Bais HaMikdash. Morning = ratzo v'shov = return from bitul. Tefillin = reshimah = "seal upon your heart." The essential receiving of Torah is by day (v'shov). Shacharis = the fullest order of prayer (Korbanos, Pesukei d'Zimrah etc.) = perfection through the reshimah.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 37–38: Mah → Me'ah; One Hundred Berachos, Shofar Sounds, and Review</div>

<div class="p">"What (mah) does Hashem ask of you?" — read not mah but me'ah (100). Mah = bitul; me'ah = 100 berachos = the entirety of prayer drawn from the reshimah. Yitzchak (= yirah/fear) found the 100 she'arim — fear drives one to bitul, bitul yields me'ah. The 100 shofar sounds on Rosh HaShanah = drawing from mah to me'ah. Reviewing one's chapter 101 times: the first study = Torah; each subsequent review introduces the hidden dimension (= prayer) into the Torah. At 100+1 reviews, the full light of the 100 berachos (= perfection of prayer) enters the Torah study, ensuring memory.</div>

<div class="ssn">§ 39: Arvis Reshus — The Circle Closes</div>

<div class="p">The halachah ends where it began. Arvis Reshus: Arvis = bitul = above Torah. The essential receiving of Torah is from the reshimah upon return (v'shov). At the level of bitul itself, obligation does not apply — it is above Torah, which is the aspect of teshuvah. Thus the entire Tishrai cycle (Rosh HaShanah through Simchas Torah) is mapped onto the single halachic fact: Tefilas Arvis Reshus.</div>

